<header><p class="st-kicker">princely state · Indian princely states</p><h1 class="st-title">Mayurbhanj State</h1><p class="st-meta">1100 CE – 1949 CE (approx.) · capital Baripada</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The largest of the Orissa princely states, ruled by the Bhanja dynasty, whose iron ore fed the Tata steel works at Jamshedpur.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhanja-dynasty">Bhanja dynasty</a> of Mayurbhanj claimed ancient descent, and tradition places its rule from around 1100, first at <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/guler-state">Haripur</a> and later at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baripada">Baripada</a>. The state lay in the forested hills of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odisha">Odisha</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Mayurbhanj had a large <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santal-people">Santal</a>, Ho and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhumij-people">Bhumij</a> population and is the home of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chhau-dance">Mayurbhanj Chhau</a> dance. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharaja">Maharaja</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sriram-chandra-bhanj-deo">Sriram Chandra Bhanj Deo</a> (r. 1882–1912) built schools and hospitals, opened a light railway and allowed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tata-steel">Tata</a> to mine the iron ore of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gorumahisani">Gorumahisani</a>, which supplied the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steel">steel</a> works founded at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamshedpur">Jamshedpur</a> in 1907.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharaja">Maharaja</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pratap-chandra-bhanj-deo">Pratap Chandra Bhanj Deo</a> set up an elected government in 1947 and held out longer than other <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odisha">Orissa</a> states, but Mayurbhanj merged into Orissa on 1 January 1949. It is now a district of Odisha.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/mayurbhanj-state.jpg" alt="Mayurbhanj State" width="320" height="231"><figcaption>Image: Great Britain India Office · Public domain · <a href="https:/